# `sweep.outcome` fixture for the cycle-time live proof (Issue #8665). Lines
# beginning with `#` are stripped by the test; `{{hours_ago:N}}` is replaced
# with an RFC3339 instant N hours before the run.
#
# Timestamps are RELATIVE on purpose. The raw ClickHouse table is created with
# the ClickStack deployment's real 168h TTL, and ClickHouse applies row TTL at
# insert time -- a fixture with fixed 2026-09-15 timestamps silently lost six of
# its seven rows the first time this ran, which is precisely the data loss this
# whole artifact set exists to survive. Every ship here therefore lands inside
# the raw retention window, and expiry is simulated later by deleting the raw
# rows outright rather than by racing a background merge.
#
# The seven envelopes, in order:
#   1. ship-alpha-101  slow, dominated by `builder` (3000s of 3600s)
#   2. ship-alpha-102  repair loop: `judge` twice (800+700) outranks `builder`,
#                      and (Issue #9443) the only ship carrying per-phase token
#                      attribution: each entry has an `attempt` index and its own
#                      `tokens_in`/`tokens_out`, except the re-judge, which is
#                      deliberately UNMEASURED (absent keys, never 0). The
#                      record's own totals exceed the measured phases by exactly
#                      `tokens_unattributed`. It exists to prove the committed
#                      extraction -- a NAMED `Array(Tuple(phase, duration_sec))`
#                      -- is unaffected by extra keys inside each entry.
#   3. ship-beta-103   slowest overall, but carries NO phase breakdown at all
#   4. ship-beta-104   a failure, with a failure_class and no PR -- not a ship
#   5. ship-alpha-105  fast, different runtime/provider/model/effort
#   6. (duplicate of 1) at-least-once delivery; must NOT double-count
#   7. ship-alpha-106  one day old, so the window spans two ISO weeks
